WEBVTT

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:16.600
a sperm whale rests after its journey from the black depths of the ocean.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:23.300
Sperm whales are the deepest diving of all mammals

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.410
and can spend up to 90 minutes on the sea floor

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.740
hunting giant squid and other bottom dwellers.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:37.940
Like mammals on land, all whales and their dolphin cousins are warm-blooded and need to breathe air.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:44.420
So how have whales adapted terrestrial organs to a life it sees so successfully?

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.900
And what prompted their ancestors to move from the land to the oceans?

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:59.890
the largest animal ever to have inhabited the planet, growing

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:10.270
up to 100 feet and weighing as much as 190 tons or 380,000 pounds. It is also extremely

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:16.640
rare. Only its need to breathe allows us a fleeting glimpse of its legendary bulk. The

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:29.960
blue whale belongs to the order Cetacea, which includes the great whales and their much

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.290
with smaller relations, porpoises and dolphins.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.530
The smallest of dolphins is a fraction of the size

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:54.810
of a blue whale's fluke.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.730
At a little over four feet, Hector's dolphins are also rare.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:07.950
Their range is limited to a few coastal areas of New Zealand

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.750
where they hunt for fish near the surface.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:29.090
As mammals, whales use lungs to breathe air.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.780
rather than gills to breathe water.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.520
Whales' lungs are relatively small,

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.680
but they are remarkably efficient.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:49.400
When a whale surfaces, it can replace up to 90%

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.680
of the air in its lungs in just a few seconds.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:56.500
But this efficiency alone cannot account

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:59.570
for its ability to stay underwater in some species.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:01.630
for as long as two hours.

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:14.800
A diving whale descends with almost half its total oxygen

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.520
stored in the muscles, where it is slowly released over time.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.960
One uses this oxygen with great economy.

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:38.400
Only essential organs, such as the brain, are supplied, while others go into oxygen debt

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.300
until the animal returns to the surface.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.690
But the deep-diving sperm whale needs more than these adaptations.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.700
Its bulbous head contains the so-called spermaceti organ,

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:19.860
filled with a complex mixture of fats and waxes.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:22.740
One of the organ's functions is, almost certainly,

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:25.420
to act as a biological ballast tank.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:29.980
When water is drawn into the huge nasal passages...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.540
the mixture cools and hardens, helping the whale to descend rapidly.

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:41.710
The process is reversed by ejecting the water

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:45.520
and pumping warm blood through the organ to liquefy the wax.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:54.650
All whales have bones that are light and filled with oil,

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:56.880
which acts as an aid to buoyancy.

00:05:58.630 --> 00:05:59.910
It's also a vital energy.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.840
reserve during periods of fasting.

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:17.780
Whales are powerful swimmers with tail flukes that are horizontal rather than vertical,

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:18.720
as in fish.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:27.100
The flukes are boneless and consist of strong fibrous tissue.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:34.570
Powered by a pair of massive muscle blocks forward of the flukes,

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:36.910
whales cruise at six miles an hour

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:41.050
with the fastest reported speed in excess of 40 miles an hour.

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:13.630
Unlike cold-blooded fish, whales need to stay warm in frigid waters.

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:21.210
Blubber, a layer of fat up to 20 inches thick, acts as thermal insulation.

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:28.810
But whales can also actively conserve heat using warm blood pumped from the heart.

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:35.930
Areas with little or no blubber, such as the head and flukes, operate a heat exchange system.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:41.560
Cooled blood in the extremities is partly heated as it passes by the warmer blood,

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:50.000
a vital adaptation because heat from a body immersed in water is lost around 25 times faster than in air.

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:59.600
The very cold waters thousands of feet below the surface are the natural hunting grounds of the sperm whale.

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:03.390
Sperm whales are the largest of the toothed whales,

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:06.850
part of the group that includes the orca, or killer whale.

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:11.750
With up to 10 or 12 pairs of cone-shaped teeth,

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:14.830
orcas can threaten even the largest prey,

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:18.600
and it's the only whale that can kill other whales.

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:35.500
Dolphins also belong to the toothed whale group and feed on fast-moving fish and squid.

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:43.320
Dolphins have even moved into some large rivers where they've become highly specialized.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:48.780
Primitive river dolphins like the Amazon Butoh have limited eyesight

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:52.180
and use echolocation to find their prey in murky waters.

00:08:55.310 --> 00:08:59.890
Echolocation, or sonar, has only been used by humans since World War II.

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:05.310
but it evolved in toothed whales and dolphins over millions of years.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:15.320
Dolphins emit short pulses of ultrasonic sound that bounce off objects,

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:20.150
producing echoes which the animal uses to create sound pictures of its surroundings.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.340
Among the constantly shifting ice flows

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:41.200
and channels of the Arctic seas,

00:09:41.770 --> 00:09:44.410
the ability to navigate beyond the limits of vision

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:46.120
is vital to survival.

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:51.820
Another toothed whale, the beluga,

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:54.500
has extraordinary sensory skills,

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:57.880
including a highly tuned sonar navigation system.

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:07.210
These complex muscles above the jaw focus a beam of sound that produces a very precise

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:12.680
image of its surroundings, enabling the whale to plot a safe route through channels under

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:13.140
the ice.

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:22.150
The belugas may descend to a thousand feet to echolocate scarce breathing holes in the

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:22.470
ice.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:27.880
Their acute hearing also helps them evade predatory polar bears.

00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:59.800
the largest whales, therefore the largest animals

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:08.200
on Earth don't have teeth at all and feed on the world's smallest animals. These zooplankton

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:19.520
in turn feed on minute sea plants called phytoplankton. Floating crustaceans known as krill are among

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:25.980
the larger zooplankton. During the summer months, their swarms form concentrations dense

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:29.120
enough to discolor surface waters for many square miles.

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:40.320
These super swarms have been estimated to weigh over 2 million tons.

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:50.490
Blue whale feeding on krill may take in 130 pounds in one mouthful,

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:55.260
and up to 4 tons a day in the richest Antarctic seas.

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:14.260
In a unique adaptation, some whales evolved baleen plates

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:25.730
simple replacement to teeth, enabling them to harvest these swarms. Rows of

00:12:25.730 --> 00:12:28.870
these plates are suspended from ridges in the upper jaw.

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:38.020
Baleen whales feed by simply opening their mouths

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:40.100
to take in both prey and water.

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:44.520
By closing their mouths and raising up the tongue,

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:51.360
water is expelled through the baleen plates filtering out the food. Baleen

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:56.940
itself is made of keratin like hair and fingernails. From hundreds of plates hang

00:12:56.940 --> 00:12:59.980
countless hair like fibers that can filter the food.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:09.840
vast quantities of food. Gray whales, the most primitive of the toothless whales, evolved thick,

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:14.980
bristly baleen to rake the seabed, dislodging crustaceans embedded in the

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:25.650
the sand. Many whales spend most of their lives feeding, but the oceans do not contain endless supplies.

00:13:33.836 --> 00:13:35.836
off the coast of Baja, California.

00:13:36.556 --> 00:13:38.796
Blue and fin whales hunt for food.

00:13:48.556 --> 00:13:49.556
in the summer months.

00:13:50.028 --> 00:13:52.448
The waters near the coast are especially plentiful.

00:13:53.324 --> 00:13:56.664
and so they and other whales congregate in large numbers

00:13:56.664 --> 00:13:59.664
to cruise among the dense shoals of fish.

00:14:32.972 --> 00:14:37.732
the whales' activities in sight of feeding frenzy among pelicans and other seabirds.

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:52.380
The fin whales feed by lunging.

00:14:52.652 --> 00:14:56.732
moving rapidly sideways through shoals of prey with mouths open.

00:14:57.932 --> 00:14:59.932
expandable throat pleats increase the

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.000
volume of water.

00:15:01.436 --> 00:15:03.556
and therefore the amount of food taken in

00:15:03.772 --> 00:15:05.172
in a single lunge.

00:15:08.252 --> 00:15:10.492
Finn whales grow to over 70 feet.

00:15:10.624 --> 00:15:13.124
on a relatively small range of foods.

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:14.420
that include krill,

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:15.120
Squid.

00:15:15.612 --> 00:15:17.612
and some small fish.

00:15:24.828 --> 00:15:28.828
Pelicans capitalize on the havoc wrought by the feeding fin whales.

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:42.850
Large whales also require enormous amounts of food because they need to build up reserves for the less productive winter season.

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:56.040
Humpbacks cooperate to harvest the sea using a skillfully woven fishing net to encircle their prey.

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:07.870
swimming well below the surface they weave a net by forcing bubbles out through their blowholes

00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:16.490
while moving in a spiral beneath a shoal of fish the bubbles drive the fish towards the center of

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:28.940
the net surging towards the surface in unison the humpbacks form an inescapable phalanx of gaping mouths

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:08.860
Orcas or killer whales cooperate in a different way.

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:23.860
Orcas can be aggressive, intelligent predators.

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:27.520
Here, they deliberately beach themselves

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:29.300
to capture young sea lions.

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:37.880
Shoreline feeding has been passed down through the generations,

00:17:38.740 --> 00:17:41.840
adults showing their young how to achieve the best results.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:07.240
This technique echoes the moment in evolutionary time

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:11.400
when the land-bound ancestors of all whales first became sea mammals.

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:18.330
Time, 55 million years ago, the drifting continents

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:20.050
were changing the face of the Earth.

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:23.880
India moved north to collide with Asia,

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:26.700
forming the massive heights of the Himalayas

00:18:26.700 --> 00:18:29.600
from what was the floor of the ancient Tethys Sea.

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:34.990
The remaining water, which today forms the Mediterranean,

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:37.130
became shallow and more saline,

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:39.220
fueling an explosion of sea life.

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:43.460
On the edges of this remnant sea,

00:18:43.540 --> 00:18:46.800
in a primeval world of tropical swamps and estuaries,

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:49.860
lived a group of animals known as creodonts.

00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:55.310
A subgroup of the creodonts, known as the Masonicids,

00:18:55.550 --> 00:18:56.730
hunted close to the water.

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:05.060
Some ate plants, but others were opportunistic scavengers,

00:19:05.540 --> 00:19:08.600
carnivores that fed on dead fish washed up by the tide.

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:37.820
Eventually, these carnivores hunted exclusively along the tide line,

00:19:38.670 --> 00:19:41.530
entering the surf to capture dead or dying prey.

00:19:44.620 --> 00:19:45.920
Within a few million years,

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:50.200
they had developed into semi-aquatic creatures feeding wholly in the sea,

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:52.600
the first primitive whales.

00:19:58.350 --> 00:19:59.930
In the last few decades...

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:05.470
scientists have gradually uncovered the mystery of how a land mammal returned to the sea near

00:20:07.830 --> 00:20:14.280
santa barbara on the californian coast new finds like this 12 million year old skull of a whale

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:19.780
are adding to an emerging picture of how whales evolved at this stage

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:29.040
scientists louise kieran and lawrence barnes are not sure exactly what is inside the rock but

00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:29.780
painstaking

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:33.960
excavation will eventually allow them to place this animal within a complex

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:39.930
evolutionary jigsaw, one that is already relatively complete.

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:59.940
The whale story begins with the amphibious animal that evolved

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.320
evolved from Masonicids.

00:21:06.740 --> 00:21:10.420
Acacetus, the earliest known whale, still had four limbs

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:14.140
as it explored its aquatic environment 50 million years ago.

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:18.740
From then on, they evolved rapidly.

00:21:19.720 --> 00:21:23.080
Ambulocetus had large hind feet clearly adapted for swimming.

00:21:24.550 --> 00:21:27.800
Then later, Rhodocetus evolved a feature that enabled them

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:29.220
to venture further from the land.

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:36.210
The tail vertebra detached from each other, allowing the tail to move up and down for propulsion.

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:42.410
Ziguriza lived around 40 million years ago.

00:21:42.980 --> 00:21:47.660
It had a flexible forelimb, but the hind limb had now almost disappeared.

00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:54.730
Ziguriza showed another important adaptation.

00:21:55.470 --> 00:21:58.090
The blowhole moved toward the top of the head.

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:07.440
In just over 10 million years, the early whales had left the land forever.

00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:16.680
These primitive whales had teeth designed to grasp and shear,

00:22:17.350 --> 00:22:22.190
but eventually they became modified to form long rows of sharp, uniform teeth.

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:29.770
Today's toothed whales, dolphins, orcas, and sperm whale, are their descendants.

00:22:35.270 --> 00:22:40.580
Around 25 million years ago, the baleen whales evolved from a toothed ancestor.

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:45.520
Even today, the fetuses of some baleen whales have remnants of teeth.

00:22:56.310 --> 00:22:59.470
For a land mammal to have evolved into the creatures we see today,

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:01.340
such as this minke whale,

00:23:02.190 --> 00:23:04.790
evolution had to be chemical as well as physical.

00:23:13.670 --> 00:23:16.750
Kidneys became specialized to deal with excess salt,

00:23:17.590 --> 00:23:19.430
fluids covered the eyes to protect them

00:23:19.430 --> 00:23:22.820
from the saline environment, and body hair was lost.

00:23:23.590 --> 00:23:25.870
No other mammal has a covering so sleek.

00:23:36.010 --> 00:23:43.260
These small adjustments taken together transformed medium-sized land mammals into huge sea creatures.

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:57.540
Just under 100 feet long, a Boeing 737 is shorter than the largest recorded blue whale.

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:03.320
A blue whale's heart is the size of a Volkswagen,

00:24:08.210 --> 00:24:11.670
and the whale itself is six times heavier than the plane.

00:24:17.180 --> 00:24:19.540
Their immense size meant that the great whales

00:24:19.540 --> 00:24:21.420
were able to fuel a hemisphere.

00:24:37.130 --> 00:24:44.070
Whale oil lit the continents for many centuries and later lubricated the machines of the industrial age.

00:24:53.630 --> 00:24:59.980
European and American whalers made huge profits, unaware they were hunting their quarry to the edge of...

00:25:00.060 --> 00:25:06.310
Whales were not only killed for their blubber.

00:25:07.060 --> 00:25:09.460
The strong and flexible baleen from their mouths

00:25:09.460 --> 00:25:11.680
was used in a range of domestic products,

00:25:11.820 --> 00:25:13.600
from corsets to umbrellas.

00:25:17.250 --> 00:25:20.450
Decade after decade, tens of thousands of whales

00:25:20.450 --> 00:25:22.470
were slaughtered to satisfy the needs

00:25:22.470 --> 00:25:24.210
of a burgeoning human population.

00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:39.680
Herman Melville's story of Moby Dick,

00:25:40.240 --> 00:25:42.300
the mythic great white sperm whale,

00:25:43.060 --> 00:25:45.240
told of high adventure, obsession,

00:25:46.030 --> 00:25:47.750
and an existence fraught with danger.

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:55.100
By the mid-19th century, whaling methods

00:25:55.100 --> 00:25:58.060
had changed little from earlier days of subsistence hunting.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:02.740
It was still an intimate struggle between men and whales.

00:26:06.980 --> 00:26:08.720
Disaster was always looming.

00:26:08.840 --> 00:26:19.130
By the turn of the 20th century,

00:26:19.660 --> 00:26:22.480
even the biggest whales were pursued in the polar regions

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:24.540
and processed in vast factories,

00:26:25.120 --> 00:26:27.700
yielding handsome profits to the new whaling magnets.

00:26:32.200 --> 00:26:36.540
Most highly prized was the fine, oily wax from the head of the sperm whale,

00:26:37.120 --> 00:26:39.580
to be used to oil precision machinery.

00:26:41.850 --> 00:26:45.520
But as catching methods changed, so did the use of the whales.

00:26:46.480 --> 00:26:51.620
Whale oil no longer lubricated the world, and carcasses were rendered down for soap,

00:26:52.150 --> 00:26:58.120
margarine, fertilizers, cosmetics, and to provide food for pets and farm animals.

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:08.880
Human attitudes have changed significantly since mass whaling ended,

00:27:09.620 --> 00:27:13.080
and whales now hold a special place in our collective conscience.

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:45.600
We've come to recognize that whales are highly intelligent,

00:27:46.220 --> 00:27:49.340
capable of sophisticated interaction with other species,

00:27:49.340 --> 00:27:52.940
like ourselves, and able to imitate and learn.

00:27:55.790 --> 00:27:57.690
Whales are even adaptable and successful

00:27:57.690 --> 00:27:59.770
in some pretty unusual situations.

00:28:15.130 --> 00:28:18.190
Whales and dolphins are essentially social animals.

00:28:21.860 --> 00:28:23.340
For some, like these dolphins,

00:28:24.050 --> 00:28:27.110
belonging to a large group has clear benefits for survival.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:31.460
of finding food are increased,

00:28:31.790 --> 00:28:34.790
since the group can range far wider than one individual.

00:28:45.220 --> 00:28:47.180
Collective defense against predators

00:28:47.180 --> 00:28:48.660
is more likely to succeed,

00:28:49.430 --> 00:28:52.470
and grouping helps bring individuals together for breeding.

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:08.610
dolphins can congregate in the hundreds their schools are fluid with individuals leaving to

00:29:08.610 --> 00:29:25.520
join other groups but with a core of members that stays together much larger whales rarely

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:29.880
school and the closest bond is probably between a solitary mother and cat

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:34.580
Baleen whales come together at feeding grounds and to mate,

00:29:35.130 --> 00:29:36.510
but then disperse rapidly.

00:29:43.270 --> 00:29:46.330
However, sperm whales, the largest of the toothed whales,

00:29:46.650 --> 00:29:49.390
do group together in pods in warmer waters.

00:29:52.120 --> 00:29:53.940
Females form the core of the pod,

00:29:54.160 --> 00:29:57.780
which also consists of calves and juveniles of both sexes.

00:30:08.760 --> 00:30:13.120
Sperm whale bulls spend most of the year alone, hunting in colder waters.

00:30:14.230 --> 00:30:17.720
But during the breeding season, they join the pod, forming a harem.

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:23.420
In this rarely filmed display of strange and sensual behavior,

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:27.950
all the sperm whales in the pod form a knot of writhing bodies.

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:41.650
Its purpose is unclear, but it may be a means of reinforcing social bonds within the pod.

00:31:04.190 --> 00:31:07.030
It may also have a more directly sexual function,

00:31:07.670 --> 00:31:10.930
since this encounter seemed to stimulate the immature males.

00:31:49.160 --> 00:31:51.500
Childed whales also live in tightly knit groups,

00:31:52.110 --> 00:31:54.650
but they are often led by a single older female.

00:31:56.080 --> 00:31:59.980
Strong social bonds like these may hold a clue to one of them.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:05.520
most perplexing mysteries about whales, their apparently deliberate strandings on shore.

00:32:08.340 --> 00:32:13.520
Evidence from stranding sites suggests that some animals had parasitic infestations of

00:32:13.520 --> 00:32:20.050
the ear and brain to such a degree that it may have caused disorientation. An infected

00:32:20.050 --> 00:32:25.180
dominant female could easily lead her entire group into dangerously shallow waters.

00:32:34.990 --> 00:32:39.050
But not all scientists agree that these infestations are the only answer.

00:32:40.030 --> 00:32:43.750
Others point to magnetite particles found in the brain of several species

00:32:43.750 --> 00:32:46.280
that may be acting as an internal compass.

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:51.410
These particles may detect the Earth's magnetic field,

00:32:51.410 --> 00:32:53.820
which originates deep within the planet,

00:32:54.160 --> 00:32:56.880
and draws an unseen grid across the globe.

00:32:58.260 --> 00:32:59.980
If whales are using this geomagnetite...

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:04.420
information to navigate, it's possible that this system breaks down,

00:33:05.880 --> 00:33:07.780
leading them into shallow water traps.

00:33:18.430 --> 00:33:20.170
Some whales look like floating reefs.

00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:25.020
They are a place in the vastness of the sea for parasites to call home.

00:33:28.210 --> 00:33:29.970
Whales are hosts to many kinds of...

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:35.990
parasites. Barnacles that attach themselves to the skin do little harm, but other creatures

00:33:35.990 --> 00:33:48.880
feed on the whale's flesh. Whale lice have claws that can cling on to a rapidly moving

00:33:48.880 --> 00:33:59.970
animal, and jaws designed to pierce its flesh. The lice may cause...

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:04.770
There's only minor irritation, but the whales' attempts to dislodge them can be spectacular.

00:34:25.330 --> 00:34:29.900
Spring arrives in the Arctic, and as the ice recedes, it's a time...

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:43.970
for renewal. Beluga whales move en masse from the ocean to the newly reopened river inlets.

00:34:54.200 --> 00:34:59.920
Whale whales shed skin and parasites during their lives, but belugas are the only whale

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:02.620
that migrate to certain areas in the Arctic,

00:35:02.840 --> 00:35:04.700
specifically to molt their skin.

00:35:04.880 --> 00:35:12.330
They wait for the tide to enter the freshwater shallows,

00:35:12.370 --> 00:35:16.210
where a gravel and sand riverbed will help them remove old skin.

00:35:31.210 --> 00:35:36.540
During the year, a coat of yellow-green algae builds up on the upper body and this too will

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:36.940
be shed.

00:35:57.840 --> 00:35:59.920
Lead groups of beluga enter the river mouth.

00:36:00.670 --> 00:36:03.970
even though they are vulnerable to polar bears in such shallow waters.

00:36:16.970 --> 00:36:23.230
As the tide peaks, hundreds of whales begin rubbing and scratching against the riverbed and each other.

00:36:33.260 --> 00:36:33.480
You

00:37:30.540 --> 00:37:34.280
After a few days, they will return to their feeding grounds in the open sea,

00:37:34.990 --> 00:37:39.940
scarred in some places, but parasite-free and with sleek white skin.

00:37:52.380 --> 00:37:54.880
With the evolution of sociability among whales

00:37:54.880 --> 00:37:57.720
came the development of highly sophisticated communication.

00:38:02.840 --> 00:38:05.000
Whales vocalize almost continuously,

00:38:05.080 --> 00:38:08.660
produce a range of sounds that is remarkably diverse.

00:38:15.370 --> 00:38:18.370
Underwater, sound travels four times faster

00:38:18.370 --> 00:38:20.420
and much further than it does in air.

00:38:22.370 --> 00:38:25.070
All whales need to emit sounds over large distances

00:38:25.070 --> 00:38:27.920
for identification and to locate other whales

00:38:27.920 --> 00:38:29.240
in the wilderness of the sea.

00:38:36.970 --> 00:38:43.040
In this way, whales constantly send and receive information about sex and activity.

00:38:48.910 --> 00:38:52.010
Berm whales use a series of cliques known as codas.

00:39:13.020 --> 00:39:18.080
It appears that one whale will call and another will answer with exactly the same coda.

00:39:19.330 --> 00:39:23.310
This will be repeated on different occasions by different individuals in the group.

00:39:26.510 --> 00:39:29.920
In other areas of the oceans, populations of sperm whales

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:33.980
have different codas, indicating the existence of dialects

00:39:33.980 --> 00:39:35.500
within the sperm whale language.

00:39:48.060 --> 00:39:50.740
One of the more remarkable features of whale communication

00:39:50.740 --> 00:39:55.150
is the plaintive, alluring song of a lone humpback whale.

00:40:08.800 --> 00:40:15.820
In an attempt to attract a mate, a male may sing for more than 24 hours with only a few short breaks to breathe.

00:40:17.220 --> 00:40:20.600
His song may last up to 35 minutes before being repeated.

00:40:22.380 --> 00:40:28.970
A whale's song is quite clearly defined with a recognizable beginning, middle, and end.

00:40:31.120 --> 00:40:33.680
Individual songs have their own peculiarities,

00:40:34.190 --> 00:40:36.430
their own syllables, phrases, and themes.

00:41:02.910 --> 00:41:04.990
Sound is one way of attracting a mate.

00:41:05.660 --> 00:41:09.460
However, for a female narwhal living in icy Arctic waters,

00:41:10.270 --> 00:41:13.270
sight takes precedence in the search for a suitable partner.

00:41:23.000 --> 00:41:25.520
Males, which grow to about 15 feet,

00:41:26.060 --> 00:41:28.740
have ivory tusks that are up to 10 feet long.

00:41:33.520 --> 00:41:36.040
Males joust with these elongated swords,

00:41:36.240 --> 00:41:39.160
which are both weapons and symbols of dominance.

00:41:39.400 --> 00:41:51.100
The successful competitor wins the right to mate with females,

00:41:51.980 --> 00:41:55.200
the strongest male passing on its genes to future generations.

00:42:12.640 --> 00:42:16.260
Among southern right whales, male competition is intense.

00:42:26.030 --> 00:42:29.450
The idea is not to be the first to mate, but the last.

00:42:30.920 --> 00:42:34.540
Because southern-right males have the largest testes on Earth,

00:42:34.940 --> 00:42:36.660
each weighing close to half a ton,

00:42:37.630 --> 00:42:41.950
the last male to mate not only deposits huge quantities of sperm,

00:42:42.530 --> 00:42:45.110
but washes out that of the preceding males.

00:42:46.090 --> 00:42:49.510
This gives his sperm the best chance to reach the female's egg.

00:43:08.670 --> 00:43:11.550
Many whales migrate huge distances to mate,

00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:14.700
but the master mariner is the great whale.

00:43:15.720 --> 00:43:17.840
Its journey is the longest of any mammal,

00:43:18.400 --> 00:43:20.680
an 11,000-mile round trip.

00:43:21.670 --> 00:43:24.350
From October, whales begin migrating south

00:43:24.350 --> 00:43:26.450
from the Bering Sea and Gulf of Alaska,

00:43:26.960 --> 00:43:28.840
hugging the North American coastline

00:43:28.840 --> 00:43:29.980
down to the warm water.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:39.380
lagoons of Baja California. The annual migrations of gray whales are

00:43:39.380 --> 00:43:45.090
synchronized with their mating patterns. Conception and birth always occur in

00:43:45.090 --> 00:43:53.030
these Baja waters, because gestation periods last 12 months. Calving every two

00:43:53.030 --> 00:43:57.170
years places enormous biological strain on those females that conceive

00:43:57.170 --> 00:43:59.980
regularly. The warm waters of

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:03.120
tropical breeding grounds may help sustain both females

00:44:03.120 --> 00:44:04.420
and their newborns.

00:44:39.440 --> 00:44:42.020
12 feet long and 1,800 pounds at birth,

00:44:42.820 --> 00:44:45.500
a young humpback grows rapidly over several months,

00:44:46.080 --> 00:44:48.440
nourished by a rich milk that contains

00:44:48.440 --> 00:44:50.600
up to 50% fat and protein.

00:44:55.240 --> 00:44:57.380
Before it weans, the young humpback

00:44:57.380 --> 00:44:59.500
may increase its weight by five times,

00:44:59.860 --> 00:44:59.980
feeding it to the young humpback.

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:02.580
eating on 150 gallons of milk each day.

00:45:06.240 --> 00:45:08.000
Apart from the occasional snack,

00:45:08.540 --> 00:45:10.900
a mother will not feed while nursing its young,

00:45:11.680 --> 00:45:14.000
even though a huge amount of energy is used up

00:45:14.000 --> 00:45:15.950
to produce such a large offspring.

00:45:25.250 --> 00:45:27.690
A mother must also protect its young from threats

00:45:27.690 --> 00:45:29.190
that can come from any direction.

00:45:30.540 --> 00:45:32.300
to a pack of hungry killer whales,

00:45:32.810 --> 00:45:35.250
any newborn whale is a tempting target.

00:45:35.470 --> 00:45:59.980
Of course,

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:04.040
Perhaps the greatest threat to all whales comes from another species of mammal altogether.

00:46:16.230 --> 00:46:20.570
Humans began whaling during the first migrations from Siberia to the Americas.

00:46:25.980 --> 00:46:29.970
These early settlers survived in a hostile environment by making...

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:35.340
full use of dead or stranded whales as a rich source of food, oil, and shelter.

00:46:37.120 --> 00:46:41.830
Recognizing the whale's value, they soon developed hunting techniques to keep themselves

00:46:41.830 --> 00:46:43.830
supplied for the long winter months.

00:46:56.530 --> 00:46:59.800
Today, subsistence whaling is still part of life.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:00.880
in the frozen north.

00:47:09.160 --> 00:47:12.200
This norwell will provide good eating for the weeks ahead.

00:47:13.130 --> 00:47:14.970
The skin and blubber will be cut and dried,

00:47:15.270 --> 00:47:17.650
providing food that is rich in vitamin C.

00:47:40.680 --> 00:47:44.160
In earlier days, the slower species that came close to the shore,

00:47:44.320 --> 00:47:46.120
such as the bowhead and right whales,

00:47:46.360 --> 00:47:50.120
were hunted in open boats using handcrafted spears.

00:47:51.390 --> 00:47:53.110
Most of the animal would have been used,

00:47:53.490 --> 00:47:56.930
including dried bones used to construct frames for shelters.

00:48:04.250 --> 00:48:07.030
The annual catch of whale populations was small,

00:48:07.580 --> 00:48:11.280
and life in the Arctic remained relatively stable for many centuries,

00:48:12.220 --> 00:48:15.810
until the coming of a new threat, the commercial whalers.

00:48:21.500 --> 00:48:25.060
But today, the mass slaughter of the great whales is all but over.

00:48:32.040 --> 00:48:35.600
Conservation groups fought long and hard to close the whaling stations,

00:48:35.640 --> 00:48:38.430
but by the time mass whaling had ended,

00:48:39.210 --> 00:48:43.600
over three-quarters of the world's population of great whales had been obliterated.

00:48:48.240 --> 00:48:53.020
Communities that once depended on the whaling trade are now sustained by a new industry,

00:48:54.160 --> 00:48:55.860
whale-watching ecotourism.

00:49:17.400 --> 00:49:20.640
Even though many of the whales are protected as endangered species,

00:49:21.500 --> 00:49:25.300
it may be a century or more before the most heavily hunted can recover.

00:49:35.050 --> 00:49:41.120
One positive step came in 1994 with the creation of a southern sanctuary in the seas surrounding

00:49:41.120 --> 00:49:46.360
Antarctica, a haven for 80% of the world's remaining great whales.

00:49:52.660 --> 00:49:57.640
During a 50-million-year odyssey, myriad sea mammals evolved from a terrestrial creature

00:49:57.640 --> 00:49:59.620
that hunted the shores of ancient oceans.

00:50:15.360 --> 00:50:18.720
They gradually lost those features that associated them with the land,

00:50:19.430 --> 00:50:25.060
now spawning their young into the oceans, where they would spend their entire lives.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:35.270
Adaptations to new food sources created a mammal without teeth, the baleen whale.

00:50:37.830 --> 00:50:46.090
New ways evolved to navigate the immensity of the sea, dive to the bottom of the ocean, and overcome the dangers of the deep.

00:50:51.730 --> 00:50:59.980
They became social animals that now hunt together, play together, and call out across the ocean.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:00.880
vastness.
